---
title: JOINs & Aggregations
description: Inner, outer, semi, and anti joins. Aggregate functions and GROUP BY.
---

# JOINs & Aggregations

## JOINs

```sql
-- Inner join
SELECT u.name, o.total FROM users u JOIN orders o ON u.id = o.user_id;

-- Left join
SELECT u.name, o.total FROM users u LEFT JOIN orders o ON u.id = o.user_id;

-- Cross join
SELECT * FROM sizes CROSS JOIN colors;

-- Semi join (EXISTS)
SELECT * FROM users u WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders o WHERE o.user_id = u.id);

-- Anti join (NOT EXISTS)
SELECT * FROM users u WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders o WHERE o.user_id = u.id);
```

JOINs work across collections of different storage types — you can join a KV collection with a strict document collection.

### Unqualified Column Names

An unqualified column in a join resolves to the one relation that has it. This applies to the SELECT list, WHERE, ON, GROUP BY, HAVING, ORDER BY, and aggregate arguments.

| The column is…                                              | Result                         |
| ----------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------ |
| Declared by exactly one relation                            | Resolves to that relation      |
| Declared by two or more relations                           | Refused: SQLSTATE `42702`      |
| Declared by none, and exactly one relation is schemaless    | Resolves to the schemaless one |
| Declared by none, and two or more relations are schemaless  | Refused: SQLSTATE `42702`      |
| Accepted by no relation                                     | Refused: SQLSTATE `42703`      |

A declared column wins over a schemaless relation. A schemaless collection accepts any field name, so when two schemaless collections are joined, NodeDB cannot know which one holds a field. The query is refused rather than reading NULL from the wrong side:

```text
ERROR:  column reference "region" is ambiguous
SQLSTATE: 42702
```

Qualify the column with its table name or alias:

```sql
-- Refused when both events and devices are schemaless
SELECT region, COUNT(*) FROM events e JOIN devices d ON e.device_id = d.id GROUP BY region;

-- Qualified: reads region from devices
SELECT d.region, COUNT(*) FROM events e JOIN devices d ON e.device_id = d.id GROUP BY d.region;
```

`USING (col)` names the column on both sides, so it is never ambiguous. Both sides must have the column.

### Derived Tables

A subquery can be a join side. It works on either side and in every join type: inner, `LEFT`, `RIGHT`, `FULL`, and `CROSS`. It can also be any side of a multi-way join.

```sql
-- Join each customer to the total of their orders
SELECT c.region, t.total
FROM customers c
JOIN (SELECT cust, SUM(amount) AS total FROM orders GROUP BY cust) t ON c.id = t.cust;

-- A derived table on the FROM side, with its own WHERE, ORDER BY and LIMIT
SELECT c.id, o.id
FROM (SELECT id FROM customers WHERE region = 'east' ORDER BY id LIMIT 10) c
JOIN orders o ON o.cust = c.id;

-- Aggregate over a join with a derived side
SELECT c.region, SUM(t.total)
FROM customers c
JOIN (SELECT cust, SUM(amount) AS total FROM orders GROUP BY cust) t ON c.id = t.cust
GROUP BY c.region;
```

- **Alias:** a derived side must have one. The alias qualifies its columns, so `t.total` reads the column the subquery projects. An unqualified column resolves to the derived side when that side is the only relation that projects it, as described above.
- **Access rules:** row-level security and redaction apply to the collections the subquery reads.
- **No correlation:** a derived side cannot refer to another relation of the same FROM clause. Such a query is refused with SQLSTATE `42601`. Use `JOIN LATERAL`, described below, to correlate a subquery with the rows before it:

```text
ERROR:  invalid reference to FROM-clause entry for table "c": a subquery in FROM cannot refer to other relations of the same query level; write JOIN LATERAL (SELECT ...) to correlate it
SQLSTATE: 42601
```

## LATERAL Joins

`LATERAL` allows the right-hand subquery to reference columns from the outer table. See [Lateral Subqueries](lateral-subqueries) for the full reference. Quick example:

```sql
-- Top 3 most recent orders for each user
SELECT u.id, u.name, o.id AS order_id, o.created_at
FROM users u
JOIN LATERAL (
    SELECT id, created_at FROM orders WHERE user_id = u.id
    ORDER BY created_at DESC LIMIT 3
) AS o ON true;

-- LEFT JOIN LATERAL — preserve users with no orders
SELECT u.id, o.last_order_at
FROM users u
LEFT JOIN LATERAL (
    SELECT MAX(created_at) AS last_order_at FROM orders WHERE user_id = u.id
) AS o ON true;
```

## Aggregate Functions

| Function                | Description             |
| ----------------------- | ----------------------- |
| `COUNT(*)`              | Row count               |
| `COUNT(DISTINCT col)`   | Distinct count          |
| `SUM(col)`              | Sum                     |
| `AVG(col)`              | Average                 |
| `MIN(col)` / `MAX(col)` | Minimum / Maximum       |

## Approximate Aggregates

Mergeable across shards, usable in continuous aggregation:

| Function                        | Description        |
| ------------------------------- | ------------------ |
| `approx_count_distinct(col)`    | HyperLogLog        |
| `approx_percentile(col, p)`     | t-digest           |
| `approx_topk(col, k)`          | SpaceSaving        |

## Weighted Random Selection

```sql
SELECT * FROM WEIGHTED_PICK('loot_table', weight => 'drop_rate', count => 1);
SELECT * FROM WEIGHTED_PICK('gacha_pool', weight => 'probability', count => 10, SEED => 'player:pull');
```
